1
00:00:03,291 --> 00:00:05,646
On 13 December 2003,

2
00:00:06,037 --> 00:00:08,784
American soldiers found
Saddam Hussein hiding

3
00:00:08,804 --> 00:00:10,919
in a hole in the ground near Tikrit.

4
00:00:12,251 --> 00:00:15,899
Less than three years later, an Iraqi
court sentenced him to death.

5
00:00:17,131 --> 00:00:19,351
This is the road between
those two moments,

6
00:00:19,603 --> 00:00:20,959
and how the world watched it.

7
00:00:25,211 --> 00:00:28,558
For eight months, the United
States military had hunted him.

8
00:00:29,911 --> 00:00:31,906
On the night of 13 December,

9
00:00:32,277 --> 00:00:35,747
about six hundred soldiers searched
two farm sites near the town

10
00:00:35,767 --> 00:00:36,639
of ad-Dawr.

11
00:00:38,112 --> 00:00:40,078
The operation was called Red Dawn.

12
00:00:41,251 --> 00:00:44,374
They had been led there by
information from people close to him,

13
00:00:44,464 --> 00:00:45,538
who had been questioned.

14
00:00:46,851 --> 00:00:49,792
They found him in a hole
six to eight feet deep,

15
00:00:50,023 --> 00:00:51,518
covered with dirt and bricks.

16
00:00:52,772 --> 00:00:53,876
He did not resist.

17
00:00:55,071 --> 00:00:58,079
The next day, in Baghdad,
the American administrator,

18
00:00:58,099 --> 00:01:02,199
Paul Bremer, told reporters, "Ladies
and gentlemen, we got him."

19
00:01:03,551 --> 00:01:05,576
In Washington, President George W.

20
00:01:05,596 --> 00:01:07,991
Bush said that Saddam
Hussein would now face,

21
00:01:08,242 --> 00:01:11,219
in his words, the justice
he denied to millions.

22
00:01:12,611 --> 00:01:15,324
He also warned that the
capture did not mean the end

23
00:01:15,344 --> 00:01:16,519
of the violence in Iraq.

24
00:01:17,771 --> 00:01:20,652
In Baghdad, some people
celebrated in the streets

25
00:01:20,823 --> 00:01:22,398
and fired guns into the air.

26
00:01:23,851 --> 00:01:26,651
In other places, including
Mosul and Fallujah,

27
00:01:26,943 --> 00:01:28,639
crowds marched in support of him.

28
00:01:29,832 --> 00:01:32,098
Across the Arab world,
the reaction was mixed.

29
00:01:33,352 --> 00:01:35,898
Jordan's government said
a page had been turned.

30
00:01:37,051 --> 00:01:41,202
A leader of Hamas,
Abdel-Aziz al-Rantissi,

31
00:01:41,222 --> 00:01:43,999
called the capture a moral
victory for President Bush.

32
00:01:48,451 --> 00:01:49,214
After the capture,

33
00:01:49,234 --> 00:01:53,279
American forces held Saddam
Hussein in a cell at Baghdad airport.

34
00:01:54,471 --> 00:01:58,093
Washington said he would be treated
under the Geneva Conventions,

35
00:01:58,545 --> 00:01:59,739
as a prisoner of war.

36
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:05,160
I think what we've said is that he is
being accorded all of the rights of a
prisoner of

37
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:09,120
war, he is being treated in accordance
with the Geneva Convention, and we
will continue

38
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:10,720
to do so.

39
00:02:11,791 --> 00:02:15,659
Iraqi leaders said he would be
tried by Iraqis, in a special court.

40
00:02:16,831 --> 00:02:21,230
From February to June 2004,
FBI agents questioned him.

41
00:02:21,681 --> 00:02:23,394
There were twenty formal interviews,

42
00:02:23,505 --> 00:02:25,819
and at least five
casual conversations.

43
00:02:27,051 --> 00:02:30,318
The agents were led by George
Piro, who spoke Arabic.

44
00:02:31,591 --> 00:02:33,746
The notes stayed secret for five years.

45
00:02:34,237 --> 00:02:36,553
They were released in July 2009,

46
00:02:36,603 --> 00:02:39,119
after requests by the
National Security Archive.

47
00:02:40,451 --> 00:02:44,382
In them, Saddam Hussein
denied any link to al-Qaeda.

48
00:02:44,402 --> 00:02:46,739
He called Osama bin Laden a zealot.

49
00:02:48,051 --> 00:02:50,867
He told the agents that Iraq
had destroyed its chemical

50
00:02:50,917 --> 00:02:53,694
and biological weapons
long before the invasion.

51
00:02:54,125 --> 00:02:55,899
No such weapons were ever found.

52
00:02:57,111 --> 00:03:00,756
He said he had let others doubt it
because he feared Iran would see Iraq

53
00:03:00,807 --> 00:03:01,298
as weak.

54
00:03:02,611 --> 00:03:05,029
He rejected the idea that
he used body doubles.

55
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:08,300
He called it, quote,
movie magic, not reality.

56
00:03:09,351 --> 00:03:13,333
He said he could remember using
a telephone only twice since 1990,

57
00:03:13,604 --> 00:03:15,479
and that he sent his orders by courier.

58
00:03:16,651 --> 00:03:18,805
On 30 June 2004,

59
00:03:19,297 --> 00:03:20,920
Saddam Hussein and eleven

60
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:24,739
of his former officials were handed
over to Iraqi legal custody.

61
00:03:26,171 --> 00:03:27,929
They were no longer prisoners of war,

62
00:03:28,181 --> 00:03:30,039
but American troops
still guarded them.

63
00:03:31,231 --> 00:03:33,950
An interim Iraqi government
had taken power that week,

64
00:03:34,120 --> 00:03:36,919
and it wanted a trial to
help build its legitimacy.

65
00:03:37,820 --> 00:03:43,540
Of course we want it to be fair, and
of course we want the world to say
we've got a fair

66
00:03:43,540 --> 00:03:50,820
trial because whatever justice has
been meted out needs to stand
international scrutiny.

67
00:03:54,891 --> 00:03:59,872
On 1 July 2004, he stood before
an Iraqi judge for the first time,

68
00:04:00,163 --> 00:04:03,019
in a courtroom in a former
palace near Baghdad airport.

69
00:04:04,292 --> 00:04:06,978
It was his first public
appearance since his capture.

70
00:04:08,291 --> 00:04:12,011
The charges read to him included
the invasion of Kuwait in 1990,

71
00:04:12,322 --> 00:04:14,518
and the killing of
Kurds and Shia in Iraq.

72
00:04:16,292 --> 00:04:19,118
He told the judge that he
was the president of Iraq.

73
00:04:20,431 --> 00:04:22,279
He called the proceedings theatre,

74
00:04:22,299 --> 00:04:24,599
and said the real criminal
was President Bush.

75
00:04:25,791 --> 00:04:29,339
He refused to sign the charge sheet
until his lawyers were present.

76
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:36,580
No, you're discussing past events. You
have to repeat your questions in the
presence of my attorneys. Otherwise, I
am not signing

77
00:04:38,191 --> 00:04:41,339
The first case against him
was about the town of Dujail.

78
00:04:42,732 --> 00:04:45,581
In July 1982, gunmen
from a Shia party,

79
00:04:45,601 --> 00:04:46,734
Dawa, had tried to kill
Saddam Hussein there.

80
00:04:49,731 --> 00:04:53,232
Prosecutors said that, in
revenge, officials detained,

81
00:04:53,322 --> 00:04:55,399
tortured and killed
people from the town.

82
00:04:56,791 --> 00:04:59,089
The court counted one
hundred forty-eight dead.

83
00:04:59,701 --> 00:05:02,019
Human Rights Watch counted
more than one hundred.

84
00:05:03,211 --> 00:05:07,511
The trial opened on 19
October 2005, in Baghdad.

85
00:05:08,042 --> 00:05:11,119
Saddam Hussein and seven
others pleaded not guilty.

86
00:05:12,331 --> 00:05:14,338
The next day, a defence lawyer for one

87
00:05:14,358 --> 00:05:17,258
of the other defendants was
taken from his office and killed.

88
00:05:18,651 --> 00:05:22,053
On 8 November, a second
defence lawyer was shot dead,

89
00:05:22,124 --> 00:05:23,518
and a colleague was wounded.

90
00:05:24,972 --> 00:05:28,198
A third defence lawyer
was killed in June 2006.

91
00:05:29,531 --> 00:05:31,626
The defence asked
for better protection.

92
00:05:32,398 --> 00:05:34,864
Human Rights Watch later
said the court had failed

93
00:05:34,884 --> 00:05:36,979
to ensure the safety
of defence lawyers.

94
00:05:38,291 --> 00:05:39,726
In January 2006,

95
00:05:39,836 --> 00:05:43,418
the judge who had opened the
trial, Rizgar Amin, resigned.

96
00:05:44,771 --> 00:05:47,239
Human Rights Watch said
that ministers and members

97
00:05:47,259 --> 00:05:50,559
of parliament had regularly
denounced the tribunal as weak.

98
00:05:51,851 --> 00:05:53,404
Sessions became chaotic.

99
00:05:54,016 --> 00:05:56,732
Saddam Hussein and his
lawyers sometimes walked out,

100
00:05:56,943 --> 00:05:59,579
or stayed away, and he
shouted at the judge.

101
00:06:00,951 --> 00:06:04,757
The first trial ended on 27 July 2006.

102
00:06:06,311 --> 00:06:08,828
On 21 August, a second trial opened,

103
00:06:08,858 --> 00:06:10,934
over the Anfal campaign
against the Kurds.

104
00:06:11,285 --> 00:06:12,619
He was a defendant in both.

105
00:06:16,911 --> 00:06:18,906
On 5 November 2006,

106
00:06:19,497 --> 00:06:22,595
the Iraqi High Tribunal found
Saddam Hussein guilty

107
00:06:22,626 --> 00:06:24,179
of crimes against humanity.

108
00:06:25,352 --> 00:06:27,277
He was sentenced to
death by hanging.

109
00:06:28,471 --> 00:06:30,629
The court said he had
ordered the killing

110
00:06:30,759 --> 00:06:33,218
of one hundred forty-eight
people in Dujail.

111
00:06:34,611 --> 00:06:35,944
Two of the other defendants,

112
00:06:36,495 --> 00:06:41,979
Barzan al-Tikriti and Awad al-Bandar,
received the same sentence.

113
00:06:43,371 --> 00:06:48,671
His former vice president, Taha Yassin
Ramadan, was sentenced to life.

114
00:06:49,362 --> 00:06:52,839
Three others received fifteen
years, and one was acquitted.

115
00:06:54,291 --> 00:06:57,049
As the sentence was read,
Saddam Hussein shouted,

116
00:06:57,260 --> 00:07:00,138
"God is greatest," and,
"Long live the nation."

117
00:07:01,671 --> 00:07:05,810
In Baghdad's Sadr City, and
in Najaf, crowds celebrated.

118
00:07:06,141 --> 00:07:09,519
In Tikrit, his supporters marched
in defiance of a curfew.

119
00:07:10,620 --> 00:07:17,860
The death sentence handed down to
Saddam Hussein pleased some people and
saddened others inside

120
00:07:17,860 --> 00:07:20,220
and outside of Iraq.

121
00:07:21,271 --> 00:07:25,778
Iraq's president, Jalal Talabani, said
that he thought the trial was fair.

122
00:07:27,271 --> 00:07:31,331
President Bush called the verdict a
milestone in the Iraqi people's efforts

123
00:07:31,682 --> 00:07:34,639
to replace the rule of a
tyrant with the rule of law.

124
00:07:36,011 --> 00:07:39,091
Human Rights Watch said the
trial was fundamentally unfair,

125
00:07:39,383 --> 00:07:41,099
and that the verdict was unsound.

126
00:07:42,371 --> 00:07:45,311
Amnesty International called
the trial deeply flawed.

127
00:07:45,883 --> 00:07:47,880
Both groups oppose
the death penalty.

128
00:07:49,032 --> 00:07:52,258
Under the court's rules, the sentence
went to an appeals chamber.

129
00:07:53,611 --> 00:07:56,851
On 26 December, the
appeals court upheld it,

130
00:07:56,882 --> 00:07:59,219
and ordered that he be
hanged within thirty days.

131
00:08:00,611 --> 00:08:05,139
On 30 December 2006, Saddam
Hussein was hanged in Baghdad.

132
00:08:06,231 --> 00:08:09,351
He was found in a hole
in December 2003.

133
00:08:09,863 --> 00:08:11,799
Three years later, he was hanged.

134
00:08:13,091 --> 00:08:14,447
Did the trial deliver justice?

135
00:08:15,140 --> 00:08:17,199
People who watched it still disagree.
